{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Far be it from me to do this.</b> For I am the judge of all the earth, and far be it from me to do violence to you. <b>And it appears</b> that Yosef's view was that the liability of the ten, when theft is found in the hand of one of them, applies only when they all gather together to steal and one of them takes the stolen object with the knowledge of all — then they are all liable; but when the theft is found in the hand of one of them, he alone is liable to death and the others are exempt, for perhaps they did not know of the theft. Therefore they said, 'we are your servants,' that is, we penalize ourselves to be servants to my lord. This is what they said 'also we,' that is, even we who are innocent and did not know of the theft, and also the one in whose hand the goblet was found — we accept upon ourselves that the judgment upon us should be equal as though we knew of the theft. And Yosef answered: far be it from me to do this, to take you as servants, for perhaps you did not know of the theft; <b>but the man in whose hand the goblet was found</b> — he alone is liable to death, and this is the force of the word 'he' (הוא); but I do not wish to put him to death, rather <b>he shall be my servant, and you</b> who are innocent — <b>go up in peace to your father.</b>" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the fact that this section is sealed with the verse 'go up in peace to your father' is an allusion to the Ten Martyrs, who went up in peace to their Father in heaven after they were purged and refined from Yosef's sin." "<b>And you already</b> know that the striped tunic (כתונת הפסים) was the beginning of the causes and the origin of the chain of events, and it was accounted to them as a great cruelty that they dipped the tunic in the blood of the goat and said to their father, 'this we have found.' Therefore the punishment came upon them in their bodies: the enslavement began with them immediately after Yosef's death, and afterward, with the Ten Martyrs who were judged in their bodies, for the body is a tunic for the soul. And therefore you will find in this section ten instances of 'the men' (האנשים), alluding to the Ten Martyrs. It should have said 'Yosef's brothers' or 'the sons of Yaakov,' but these are the instances: ויקחו האנשים את המנחה הזאת, הבא את האנשים, כי אתי יאכלו האנשים, ויבא האיש את האנשים, וייראו האנשים, ויבא האיש את האנשים, ויתמהו האנשים, אמתחות האנשים, והאנשים שלחו, אחרי האנשים — behold, ten instances of אנשים. And these are the Ten Martyrs: Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, Rabbi Yishmael ben Elisha the High Priest, Rabbi Akiva ben Yosef, Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava, Rabbi Chananya ben Tardyion, Rabbi Yeshavav the Scribe, Rabbi Elazar ben Dama, Rabbi Chanina ben Chakhinai, Rabbi Chutzfit the Translator, R. Elazar ben Shamua. Of them David said, peace be upon him (Psalms 9:13): כי דורש דמים אותם זכר לא שכח צעקת ענוים. And you should know that they were not all in the same time, nor were they all sentenced together." "<b>And so</b> you will find in the chapters of the Hekhalot: Said Rabbi Yishmael — that day was Thursday when the report came from the great city of Rome, that Lupinus the Caesar commanded, and they seized these four men from the mighty of Israel: Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, Rabbi Yishmael ben Elisha, Rabbi Elazar ben Dama, and Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava, along with two hundred and eight thousand disciples of the sages from Jerusalem, their ransom. And when R. Nechunya ben HaKana saw this decree, he arose and brought me down to the Chariot, and I asked a question of Suryael the Prince of the Countenance, and he said to me: Ten were written in the heavenly court and given to Samael the wicked, the prince of Esav, and they said to him: Go and destroy from the mighty of Israel every good portion, thigh and shoulder, to complete the decree of (Exodus 21:16) וגונב איש ומכרו ונמצא בידו מות יומת, and it is held in store for him to take vengeance upon him until there arrives (Isaiah 24:21) יפקד ה׳ על צבא המרום במרום — the kids and lambs of the day of first-fruits. Said Rabbi Yishmael: All these warnings and all these conditions — they warned him and stipulated with Samael the wicked, and he said: I accept it upon myself. And he chose ten from the mighty of Israel. Said Rabbi Yishmael: What did Zohuriel, HaShem the Elohim of Israel, do at that hour? He did not have time to say to the scribe: Write of decrees and great, harsh, fierce, hasty, terrible, and heavy plagues upon Rome, because of the wrath that was filled against Samael the wicked for accepting all these conditions; rather, immediately he took parchment and wrote upon it: This vengeance is destined and held in store for wicked Rome. A cloud will rise and stand above Rome for six months, and a moist eruption will descend upon man and beast, upon silver and gold, upon fruits and all kinds of metals. And afterward another cloud will rise and displace its fellow and stand in its place for six other months, and the plague of leprosy, scaly eruptions, bright spots, and all kinds of afflictions will descend upon wicked Rome, until the hour comes when a man will say to his fellow: Take wicked Rome for a penny, it and all that is in it, and he will answer him: It is not wanted by me. Thus far in the chapters of the Hekhalot." "<b>But</b> one should wonder at what is written there, that Rabbi Chanina ben Tardyion was substituted for Lupinus the Caesar, and Lupinus the Caesar is the one who was burned, and likewise all the others, for so it is written there — and the same measure applies to all the sages of Israel. And I have heard on this matter that just as Yitzchak was substituted by the ram, and reward was established for him as though he had been offered and as though his ashes were piled upon the altar, so the sages of Israel were substituted by others; and since they tasted the taste of death in that they were seized and their sentence was completed for this, they received their punishment, and it is as though they were killed. And what the verse says, 'and put each man's silver in the mouth of his sack,' alludes to this matter, for the verse likened the soul to a bound sum of silver (כסף צרור) and the body to a sack (אמתחת), for it is the sack of the soul. And likewise, in my opinion, what was mentioned above, 'for now we return this time' (כי עתה שבנו זה פעמים), alludes to what the verse says (Job 36:36) על תשובות באנשי און, and it is written (Job 33:29) פעמים שלש עם גבר, and this is the speech of Yehuda, who began with the matter of yibum first. And understand well, for from this he mentioned at the end 'and they returned to the city' (וישובו העירה), where it could have said וישובו מצרימה, but it is from the language of 'a small city' (עיר קטנה), and the discerning will understand. And because of this the section was sealed." "<b>And you, go up in peace to your father.</b> He could have said 'go in peace on your way,' but the verse is explicitly about Joseph's brothers for the hour, and alludes to the ten martyrs of the kingdom, throughout the generations, who went up in peace to their Father in heaven. And thus the masters of truth, of blessed memory, expounded concerning the ten martyrs of the kingdom: no creature can stand in their presence; and likewise Scripture says (Isaiah 64:3): ״עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך יעשה למחכה לו״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:39.512467056Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}